canonical_claim.statement:
Printed statements refuted. Proposition 6.2 (p. 14): 'Suppose $m < n \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, and fix potential outcomes $\boldsymbol{y}$ and $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Then $\mathbb{P}(\tau(\boldsymbol{y}) \in \mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z})) \ge 1 - \alpha$, (6.3) where the probability is with respect to the variable $\boldsymbol{Z}$.' Appendix D (p. 29): '$\mathcal{J}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z}) \subset \mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z})$, (D.3)' and '$\mathbb{P}(|T(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}},\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Z}}) - \tau_0| \ge |T(\boldsymbol{Y}',\boldsymbol{Z}) - \tau_0|) \le \mathbb{P}(|T(\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Y}},\widetilde{\boldsymbol{Z}}) - \tau_0| \ge |T(\boldsymbol{Y}^{(+)},\boldsymbol{Z}) - \tau_0|) < \alpha$, (D.7) so $\boldsymbol{w}$ is incompatible with $(\boldsymbol{Y}',\boldsymbol{Z})$, as desired.'

Counterexample (the adjudicated validator's; exact rational arithmetic). $n = 20$, $m = 3$, $\alpha = 109/500$. Potential outcomes $(y_i(1), y_i(0))$: $(1,1)$ for units $1,\ldots,11$; $(0,1)$ for units $12,13,14$; $(0,0)$ for units $15,\ldots,20$. Count vector $(v_{11},v_{10},v_{01},v_{00}) = (11,0,3,6)$ and $\tau(\boldsymbol{y}) = -3/20$. $\boldsymbol{Z}$ is uniform over the $\binom{20}{3} = 1140$ assignments. Missingness rule (a deterministic function of $\boldsymbol{Z}$): $J_1 = 1$ exactly when the treated set is $\{12,13,14\}$, and $J_i = 0$ for every other unit and every other assignment. $\boldsymbol{M}$ is defined by (6.1). Then:
(i) every hypothesis of Proposition 6.2 and of Section 6 holds: $0 < m < n$, $\alpha \in (0,1)$, $\boldsymbol{y}$ is a table of binary potential outcomes, $\boldsymbol{J} \in \{1,0\}^n$ is a random vector that depends on $\boldsymbol{Z}$;
(ii) at the assignment with treated set $\{12,13,14\}$ the complete observed count vector is $\boldsymbol{n} = (n_{11},n_{10},n_{01},n_{00}) = (0,3,11,6)$; unit 1 is a control with outcome 1 and is missing, so $\boldsymbol{Y}^{(-)} = \boldsymbol{Y}$ with count vector $(0,3,11,6)$ and $\boldsymbol{Y}^{(+)}$ has count vector $(0,3,10,7)$; $T(\boldsymbol{Y}^{(-)},\boldsymbol{Z}) = -11/17$ and $T(\boldsymbol{Y}^{(+)},\boldsymbol{Z}) = -10/17$;
(iii) Algorithm 2.3 gives $\mathcal{I}_\alpha(0,3,11,6) = [-7/10, -3/20]$ and $\mathcal{I}_\alpha(0,3,10,7) = [-13/20, -1/5]$, so by (6.2) $\mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z}) = [\min(-7/10, -11/17), \max(-1/5, -10/17)] = [-7/10, -1/5]$, which does not contain $\tau(\boldsymbol{y}) = -3/20$;
(iv) (D.3) fails at this assignment: $\boldsymbol{Y} \in \mathcal{Y}(\boldsymbol{M})$ and $-3/20 \in \mathcal{I}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{Y},\boldsymbol{Z}) \subset \mathcal{J}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z})$, but $-3/20 \notin \mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z})$;
(v) the final inequality of (D.7) fails with $\boldsymbol{w} = \boldsymbol{y}$, $\tau_0 = -3/20$, $\boldsymbol{Y}' = \boldsymbol{Y}$: (D.5) holds since $-3/20 > \max(-1/5, -10/17)$, and both probabilities in (D.7) equal $249/1140 = 83/380 > 109/500$. The table $\boldsymbol{y}$ is possible given $(\boldsymbol{Y},\boldsymbol{Z})$ and is not possible given $(\boldsymbol{Y}^{(+)},\boldsymbol{Z})$; among the count vectors possible given $(0,3,10,7)$ with $\tau \ge -3/20$ the largest p-value is $248/1140 < 109/500$, attained at $(8,0,3,9)$ and $(9,0,3,8)$;
(vi) coverage. Grouping the 1140 assignments by complete observed count vector $\boldsymbol{n}$ (number of assignments; $\mathcal{I}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{n})$; whether it contains $-3/20$): $(0,3,11,6)$: 1, $[-7/10,-3/20]$, yes; $(0,3,12,5)$: 18, $[-3/4,-3/10]$, no; $(0,3,13,4)$: 45, $[-4/5,-7/20]$, no; $(0,3,14,3)$: 20, $[-17/20,-2/5]$, no; $(1,2,11,6)$: 33, $[-11/20,1/20]$, yes; $(1,2,12,5)$: 198, $[-3/5,0]$, yes; $(1,2,13,4)$: 165, $[-13/20,-1/20]$, yes; $(2,1,11,6)$: 165, $[-7/20,1/4]$, yes; $(2,1,12,5)$: 330, $[-2/5,1/5]$, yes; $(3,0,11,6)$: 165, $[-1/10,2/5]$, no. In each group $T(\boldsymbol{n}) \in \mathcal{I}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{n})$, so on the 1139 assignments with nothing missing $\mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z}) = \mathcal{I}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{Y},\boldsymbol{Z})$. Hence $\mathbb{P}(\tau(\boldsymbol{y}) \in \mathcal{I}^{\circ}_\alpha(\boldsymbol{M},\boldsymbol{Z})) = (33+198+165+165+330)/1140 = 891/1140 = 297/380 < 391/500 = 1 - \alpha$, a shortfall of $1/2375$ (with complete data the coverage is $892/1140$).
